<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Ziębice</h1><p class="st-meta">1322 CE – 1791 CE (approx.) · capital Ziębice</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Lower Silesian duchy, known in German as Münsterberg, that passed from the Piasts to the Poděbrady and Auersperg families before being sold to Prussia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1322 the sons of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolko-i-the-strict">Bolko I of Świdnica</a> divided his lands, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolko-ii-of-ziebice">Bolko II</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziebice">Ziębice</a> (Münsterberg), south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, as a separate duchy split from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-swidnica">Duchy of Świdnica</a>. Bolko II resisted Bohemian overlordship longer than most Silesian dukes but did homage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-bohemia">King John of Bohemia</a> in 1336.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchy was a modest agrarian territory of small towns and villages. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast</a> line ended in 1428, and in 1456 the duchy passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-podebrady">George of Poděbrady</a>, later king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a>, whose sons held it as dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziebice">Münsterberg</a> and introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. In 1654 the emperor granted it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-auersperg">Auersperg family</a>, who seldom lived there.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Silesia</a> came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1742, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-auersperg">Auerspergs</a> kept the ducal title and estates. In 1791 they sold their remaining Silesian holdings to the Prussian crown, ending the duchy. The town, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziebice">Ziębice</a>, became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1945.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-ziebice.jpg" alt="Duchy of Ziębice" width="320" height="384"><figcaption>Image: Tymoteusz Trzewiczek · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%9Al%C4%85sk_1322-1331.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1322 CE – 1791 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ziębice</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bolko II, Henry the Elder of Münsterberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Münsterberg, Duchy of Munsterberg, Duchy of Ziebice</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1556&amp;focus=duchy-of-ziebice" aria-label="Duchy of Ziębice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5069 421 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Ziębice lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5389 677L5375 670M5389 677L5330 692M5389 677L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5375 670h0M5330 692h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5389 677h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5389 677h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ziębice. <a href="/atlas?year=1556&amp;focus=duchy-of-ziebice">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
